	<section class="hero alt-hero">
		<div class="hero-badge">Comparison · Self-Hosted Email</div>
		<h1>Mailcow <span>vs</span> Mail-in-a-Box</h1>
		<p class="alt-lead">
			Two of the most popular ways to self-host email, with the same Postfix + Dovecot core
			and opposite philosophies. Mail-in-a-Box is a one-command box for a fresh Ubuntu
			22.04 machine that runs on 1 GB of RAM and deliberately has almost nothing to
			configure. Mailcow is an 18-service Docker groupware stack with a comprehensive admin
			UI, Rspamd and ClamAV, and a 6 GiB RAM floor. Neither ships a transactional sending
			API. If that's the gap, we're a third option, covered below.
		</p>
		<div class="alt-meta" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
			<span>Last updated <time datetime={dateModified}>8 October 2026</time></span>
			<span>·</span>
			<span>By the Vectis Mail team</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
		<h2>At a glance</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Side-by-side, current as of October 2026 (Mailcow 2026-09a, Mail-in-a-Box v77).</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Mailcow</th><th>Mail-in-a-Box</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mail stack</th><td>Postfix · Dovecot</td><td>Postfix · Dovecot</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Deployment</th><td>Docker Compose, 18 services</td><td>One setup script, installs onto the OS</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Supported OS</th><td>Any Linux running Docker (full VMs; not LXC/OpenVZ)</td><td>Ubuntu 22.04 LTS only, fresh machine</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Minimum RAM</th><td>6 GiB + 1 GiB swap</td><td><strong>512 MB (1 GB recommended)</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Antispam / antivirus</th><td><strong>Rspamd + ClamAV</strong></td><td>SpamAssassin + Postgrey greylisting</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Webmail</th><td>SOGo</td><td>Roundcube</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Calendar / contacts</th><td>Yes (SOGo)</td><td>Yes (Nextcloud)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Exchange ActiveSync</th><td>Yes (SOGo)</td><td>Yes (Z-Push)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">DNS</th><td>You run DNS elsewhere</td><td><strong>Runs its own authoritative DNS</strong> (or use external DNS)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Backups</th><td>Helper script you schedule</td><td><strong>Built in, nightly</strong> (local, rsync, S3, B2)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Customisation</th><td><strong><code>mailcow.conf</code>, per-service overrides, web UI</strong></td><td>None by design ("no user-configurable setup options")</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Admin API</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (control-panel actions)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Transactional sending API</th><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Update model</th><td>Manual <code>update.sh</code></td><td>Re-run the setup script</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Automatic rollback</th><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">License</th><td>GPL-3.0</td><td>CC0 1.0 (public domain)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Pricing</th><td>Free (paid ServerCow support optional)</td><td>Free (donations)</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Philosophy: appliance vs platform</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box's README states it plainly: there are no user-configurable setup
			options; it "just works." You give it a fresh Ubuntu 22.04 machine and a hostname, and
			it configures mail, webmail, calendar, DNS, TLS, backups, a firewall and monitoring
			for you, making every decision on your behalf. That's the whole point: the fewer
			knobs, the less there is to get wrong.
		</p>
		<p>
			Mailcow is a platform you operate. It ships sensible defaults, but everything is
			adjustable through <code>mailcow.conf</code>, per-service override files and a
			comprehensive web UI covering domains, mailboxes, DKIM, quarantine, rate limits and
			SOGo groupware. More to learn, much more you can shape.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Hardware and operating system</h3>
		<p>
			This is often the deciding factor. Mail-in-a-Box needs at least 512 MB of RAM (1 GB
			recommended) and will happily run on the cheapest VPS tier. Mailcow's documented
			minimum is 6 GiB of RAM plus 1 GiB of swap with the default configuration, rising to
			8 GiB for about 5 to 10 users and roughly 16 GiB for a company with 15 ActiveSync
			phones, because it also runs SOGo, ClamAV, a full-text search engine and MariaDB.
		</p>
		<p>
			On the OS side, the constraint runs the other way. Mail-in-a-Box's setup script
			refuses to install on anything except Ubuntu 22.04, so it can't use Ubuntu 24.04 or
			any other distribution. Mailcow runs wherever Docker does on full virtualisation (KVM,
			ESX, Hyper-V), on x86_64 or ARM64, but not inside LXC or OpenVZ containers or on a
			NAS.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">DNS</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box is unusual in running its own authoritative DNS server. Point your
			domain's nameservers at the box and it publishes SPF, DKIM, DMARC, DNSSEC, DANE TLSA,
			MTA-STS and SSHFP records automatically, which removes the most error-prone part of
			running mail. If you'd rather keep DNS with your registrar, it lists the records to
			copy across. Mailcow assumes you run DNS elsewhere and shows you the records to
			create.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Spam filtering</h3>
		<p>
			Mailcow ships Rspamd, a modern scoring engine with DKIM signing, DMARC checks and a
			web interface, plus ClamAV for attachment scanning. Mail-in-a-Box uses the older
			SpamAssassin together with Postgrey greylisting, and doesn't include an antivirus
			scanner.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Groupware</h3>
		<p>
			Both give you calendar, contacts and phone sync out of the box, through different
			components. Mailcow's SOGo is one integrated groupware web app: webmail, CalDAV/CardDAV
			and Exchange ActiveSync together. Mail-in-a-Box assembles the same capabilities from
			Roundcube webmail, Nextcloud for CalDAV/CardDAV and Z-Push for ActiveSync.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Backups and updates</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box takes a nightly backup automatically with Duplicity, to local disk or
			to remote storage over rsync, S3 or Backblaze B2, with no setup beyond choosing a
			target. Mailcow ships a <code>backup_and_restore.sh</code> helper script that you
			run or schedule yourself.
		</p>
		<p>
			Neither has an automated update safety net. Mail-in-a-Box upgrades by re-running its
			setup script, which works best on a box nobody has customised. Mailcow upgrades with
			<code>update.sh</code>, which pulls new images and restarts containers. Neither takes
			a pre-update snapshot, runs a health-check gate or rolls back on failure: if an update
			breaks something, recovery is manual.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Programmatic sending</h3>
		<p>
			Neither ships a transactional sending API. Both expose an admin API for managing
			users and aliases, but sending from an application means SMTP submission as a real
			mailbox with its own username and password. There are no domain-scoped API keys, no
			inbound webhooks and no per-domain analytics in either.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Who each is best for</h2>
		<div class="feature-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<div class="feature-card">
				<h3>Choose Mailcow if</h3>
				<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
					<li>You have 6–8 GiB of RAM to give it and want a full groupware suite.</li>
					<li>You want Rspamd and ClamAV filtering rather than SpamAssassin.</li>
					<li>You need to customise the stack and manage it from one admin UI.</li>
					<li>You need a current OS: Ubuntu 24.04, Debian or ARM64.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="feature-card">
				<h3>Choose Mail-in-a-Box if</h3>
				<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
					<li>You're running mail for yourself, a family or a small organisation.</li>
					<li>Your budget is a 1 GB VPS.</li>
					<li>You want DNS, TLS and backups handled automatically.</li>
					<li>You'd rather have no settings at all than the wrong ones.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The third option: Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			If what's missing from <em>both</em> is a transactional sending API, a modern OS on
			a mid-size VPS, or an update you don't have to supervise, that's the gap Vectis Mail
			is built to close. It's self-hosted on the same proven Postfix and Dovecot core, with
			Rspamd by default, installed by one guided command:
		</p>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				<li>Runs on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS from 2 GB of RAM without ClamAV (4 GB recommended), so it's lighter than Mailcow and not tied to an older Ubuntu release.</li>
				<li>Domains, mailboxes, aliases and API keys managed from an admin dashboard or the REST API, taking effect immediately with no restarts.</li>
				<li>A 40+ endpoint REST API: transactional sending (single and batch, with attachments), HMAC-signed inbound webhooks, domain-scoped API keys with per-domain rate limits, and analytics.</li>
				<li>Atomic 6-phase updates (snapshot, migrate, pull, deploy, health-check, complete) with automatic rollback, and <code>vectis verify</code> to prove the box runs exactly what was published.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
			To be equally clear about where Vectis Mail doesn't fit: it's Docker-only, and it
			doesn't yet ship calendar, contacts or ActiveSync; those are on the roadmap, not
			shipped today. If groupware is a day-one requirement, pair it with a dedicated
			CalDAV/CardDAV service. See the dedicated <a href="/alternatives/mailcow/">Vectis
			Mail vs Mailcow</a> and <a href="/alternatives/mail-in-a-box/">Vectis Mail vs
			Mail-in-a-Box</a> comparisons for the full detail on each.
		</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail free</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View pricing</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Mailcow or Mail-in-a-Box better?</h3>
				<p>
					They're built for different jobs. Mail-in-a-Box is a one-command personal or
					small-organisation mail server for a fresh Ubuntu 22.04 machine, with almost
					nothing to configure. Mailcow is a full Docker groupware stack (SOGo, Rspamd,
					ClamAV) with a comprehensive admin UI and room to customise, at the cost of a
					much heavier footprint: 6 GiB RAM plus 1 GiB swap minimum. Pick on hardware
					budget and how much control you want, not on mail quality: both deliver mail
					through the same Postfix and Dovecot core.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How much RAM do Mailcow and Mail-in-a-Box need?</h3>
				<p>
					Mail-in-a-Box needs at least 512 MB and recommends 1 GB; its installer warns
					below 768 MB. Mailcow's documented minimum is 6 GiB RAM plus 1 GiB swap with
					the default configuration, with 8 GiB recommended for roughly 5 to 10 users,
					because it also runs SOGo, ClamAV, a full-text search engine and MariaDB.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Mail-in-a-Box run on Ubuntu 24.04?</h3>
				<p>
					No. As of Mail-in-a-Box v77 (September 2026), its setup script checks for
					Ubuntu 22.04 and refuses to install on anything else, including Ubuntu 24.04.
					Mailcow runs on any Linux host that runs Docker on full virtualisation (KVM,
					ESX, Hyper-V), on x86_64 or ARM64, but not inside LXC or OpenVZ containers.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Do Mailcow and Mail-in-a-Box both have calendar and contacts?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Mailcow bundles SOGo, which provides webmail, CalDAV/CardDAV calendar and
					contacts, and Exchange ActiveSync. Mail-in-a-Box bundles Nextcloud for
					CalDAV/CardDAV and Z-Push for Exchange ActiveSync, alongside Roundcube webmail.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does either have a transactional sending API?</h3>
				<p>
					No. Both have an administrative API for managing users and aliases, but
					neither ships a REST API for programmatic sending, inbound webhooks, or
					per-domain analytics. Sending from an application means SMTP submission as a
					real mailbox. Vectis Mail is a self-hosted platform built to add that sending
					API alongside mailbox hosting.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

